WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:13.180
When you look out at a body of water--whether
it's a pond, river, ocean, even your own bathtub-

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.570
you are peering into the home of countless
microorganisms.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.560
Some are simple, others more complex.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.990
And perhaps among the most stunning are diatoms,
though they are probably not in your bathtub.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:38.220
These are photosynthetic, single-celled eukaryotes
whose silica shells render them almost like jewels.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.640
Their sparkly and distinctive appearance have
captivated microbiologists since the earliest

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.600
days of the field in the 18th century.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.969
But we suspect that most of you haven’t
heard of Bacillaria, one of the earliest diatoms

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:54.340
described in the scientific literature.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.410
They’re like one of those deeply specific
niches you find when you’re exploring a

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.970
new hobby, one of those narrow obsessions
that you had no clue existed until you happened

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:11.520
upon a forum about 1990s Swedish hip hop that
goes back 300 pages.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.290
We have not stumbled on any 300 page bacillaria
forums (though we are open to suggestions

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.860
if you know of a good one).

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:27.150
But it does seems like people who are interested
in bacillaria are really interested in bacillaria,

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.920
including one notable, drunken attempt to
mimic their behavior, as humans.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.430
We’ll get to that more in a bit.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.950
The source of the fascination lies in the
mystery and contradictions of the microbe’s

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:48.430
movement, which has so mesmerized us that
it may have obscured some other truths about

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.560
the organism.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.180
Let's observe this dance from the cheap seats,
fully zoomed out, so we can watch them perform

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.220
across this microscopic stage.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:09.770
The Bacillaria paxillifer begins compact,
but it quickly extends in both directions.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:10.770
And extends.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:16.520
And extends some more until it is stretched
across a good portion of the screen.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:33.880
Then it contracts back inward, back into that
more discreet state, only to renew that dance

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.860
again, this time reaching only in one direction.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:44.439
It's a remarkable feat of microscopic acrobatics,
taking the organism across the stage even

00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:46.659
as it stays in one spot.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:54.260
In fact, this Bacillaria's movement is so
coordinated it almost seems like a worm,

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:00.800
a weird one, like a pokey abstraction of the nematode squirming around next to it.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:09.489
The first person to describe Bacillaria paxillifer
and its dance was Otto Friedrich Müller in 1783.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:12.719
He described the organism as a "peg-animal".

00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:18.760
The pegs, as he saw them, were inflexible
cylinders that contained some sort of yellow

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.409
creature or film.

00:03:20.409 --> 00:03:24.829
When the organism was at rest, the pegs formed
a quadrilateral.

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:30.040
And when it moved, the outer pegs began to
slide, followed by their neighbor until a

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.450
whole sequence of pegs slid out into a line.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:40.069
Remember, this was the earliest days of our
understanding of the microcosmos, when we

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:46.379
still thought of life as being divided only
between plants and animals.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:52.620
And based on his observations, Müller struggled
with an important question: were the Bacillaria

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:59.090
he was looking at a single animal, or was
it some kind of animal group that came together

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.469
and coordinated this incredible formation?

00:04:03.469 --> 00:04:11.230
He debated himself back and forth, ultimately
deciding that this must be just one single animal.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:16.950
After all, he could think of no parallel example
of such synchronized movement among animals

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.750
in the macro world.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:23.640
It seemed improbable that nature would conceive
of such a thing at any level, and honestly,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.250
it still does.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:31.940
While Müller acknowledged that the microcosmos
was full of strange things, in the framework

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:39.340
of how we understood nature at that time,
it seemed to him that this must be one animal--an

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.350
animal he named Vibrio paxillifer.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.350
Despite the value of his observations and
his reasoning, Müller was, of course, wrong.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.690
The strange animal he had seen was actually
not one animal.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.390
It was not even an animal at all.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:02.700
Those pegs were each microscopic individuals
that had assembled into that larger entity,

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:08.000
and through various reclassifications over
the years, Vibrio paxillifer eventually became

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.970
known as Bacillaria paxillifer, a cosmopolitan
diatom found in both marine and freshwater

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.530
environments.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:22.560
Each Bacillaria colony is actually the product
of a series of divisions, meaning that this

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.480
whole ensemble is made up of clones.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:32.530
The individual cells have a slit called the
raphe, through which they excrete a kind of

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:38.240
adhesive substance that keeps them attached
to their neighboring sister cell.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.470
The movements of the colony are likely driven
by light.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:46.470
At night, they’re usually in their compact
state, remaining still.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.180
And during the day, they stay extended.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:56.850
The cycle of expansion and contraction over
and over, that’s mostly observed at dawn and dusk.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:04.010
Bacillaria paxillifer is also known as Bacillaria
paradoxa, which is a fitting name because

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:09.470
despite all of the mechanisms we've been able
to decipher, there are many questions left

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.430
to understand.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:17.490
There's no obvious method of communication
between cells or obvious moving parts within

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.030
each one, so how do they synchronize their
movement?

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.020
How do they understand where their neighbors
are?

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.020
These are the same questions you might ask
while you're watching a talented ensemble

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:36.560
of dancers, where each individual's movements
are perfectly aligned through intricate choreography,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:42.000
but that display is a feat of training and
precision, guided by music and internalized

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.990
by each performer.

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:46.370
These are microbes.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:48.360
What directs them?

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:54.140
That question has been the basis of many a
diatomist's fascination with Bacillaria for

00:06:54.140 --> 00:07:01.340
centuries, a fascination that has preoccupied
minds and inspired creative feats.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:07.580
In his description of them, Müller took 10
pages to argue with himself over whether this

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.680
kind of coordinated movement between discrete
organisms was even possible.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:18.810
And we were able to read his words in English
because they were translated from the original

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.210
Danish back in 2005.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:27.140
The translator noted that this activity they
were engaged in was supposed to be a fun,

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:29.100
relaxing side project.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:35.880
A side project that involved having to read
Gothic lettering, process 18th century, scholarly

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:42.700
Danish diction, and contend with the physical
challenges of decaying, old books.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:49.560
All that work just to understand the first
words used to describe this species, the foundational

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:56.830
text as it were for an ongoing tradition of
Bacillaria observation and obsession.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:02.760
There are whole fandoms, lovely as they are,
built on much less effort.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:10.830
There's also the story of the 1987 North American
Diatom Symposium, where around 30 people attempted

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:15.890
their own transformational work: the Bacillaria
Shuffle.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:24.710
Alcohol was involved, as was a chant that
went, "Do the bacillaria, do the bacillaria."

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:31.520
In the words of someone who was there, the
dance was marked by a "general lack of physical

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.839
coordination of the individual persons."

00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:39.669
You could take that as a condemnation of the
people involved, or as an homage to the much

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:45.160
simpler and yet much more synchronized organism
who inspired them.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:54.100
But in focusing so much on this movement,
it's taken time to uncover other secrets of

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.689
the Bacillaria.

00:08:55.689 --> 00:09:01.240
Since the days of Müller, scientists have
thought that there was just one species distributed

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.249
in all sorts of waters, all around the world.

00:09:04.249 --> 00:09:09.290
After all, how many species could there be
exhibiting such a complicated way of life?

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:18.170
But in 2007, more than 200 years after Bacillaria
had first been described, a scientist studied

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:24.129
the internal structures of the organism and
realized that there are actually several distinct

00:09:24.129 --> 00:09:26.899
species distributed in different waters.

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:34.069
Müller, the original Bacillaria admirer,
wrote of what he saw: “it is in the microscopical

00:09:34.069 --> 00:09:40.879
world that such unusual sights most often
meet.”

00:09:40.879 --> 00:09:46.459
The strange dance he observed would go on
to underpin his incorrect classification of

00:09:46.459 --> 00:09:47.459
the organism.

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:55.060
And over time, diatom enthusiasts would continue
to be so fixated on this collective behavior,

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:59.749
that it would take time to notice the individuality
of its varied species.

00:09:59.749 --> 00:10:05.600
You could almost write a fable around our
observations of the organism, with a simple

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.670
moral to not be so distracted by beauty.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:16.389
But that feels a little hypocritical on our
part given that we savor the diversion of

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:18.699
the microcosmos’ allure.

00:10:18.699 --> 00:10:24.540
The artistry of nature, whether large or small,
has always been an inspiration for scientific

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:29.620
inquiry, and as researchers come to better
understand Bacillaria, there will only be

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:35.339
new sources of charm and beauty to discover.

00:10:35.339 --> 00:10:41.510
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:46.230
And thank you, of course, to all of the people
who make this show possible.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:51.470
If you like it, these are the people to thank,
our patrons on Patreon.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.260
Thank you so much to all of you.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:58.420
If you want to check it out, and help us make
more good content, maybe get some better equipment,

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:02.870
you can find us at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:06.950
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss, you can check out

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:08.779
Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram.

00:11:08.779 --> 00:11:13.379
And if you want to see more from us, there’s
